+ {"id":"CF-177","title":"P4 - the corrected admission paragraph pins another repository's line ranges but no scheduler commit, and laneAutoArm's cited range ends on the file's last line","status":"pending","priority":"P4","complexity":1,"created_at":"2026-11-04T07:54:05Z","updated_at":"2026-11-04T07:54:05Z","detail":{"fingerprint":"b6332f6e6041090c57d70962bf305bbf6ca0898b9dc11819bd59f1a9def84297"},"reasoning":"The corrected paragraph cites the scheduler's source by bare line ranges (internal/api/lane_onboarding.go:269-271, :303-310, :389-403 and internal/database/models.go:39,47-52) into a DIFFERENT repository, and names no commit anywhere in the shipped file: the pin exists only in the evidence artifact (bf7427c8). Every number in that sentence is therefore only reproducible while coding-hermes/scheduler origin/main happens to equal the tree the docs tick read — which is true today and is luck, not a contract. laneAutoArm's cited range 389-403 is the whole tail of a 403-line file, so the first function appended after it silently re-assigns the citation to other code. The reviewer who re-checks the paragraph a month from now has two failure modes and no way to tell which one they are in.\n\nDistinct from CF-095, where the artifact is pinned and the tip it names fails to reproduce: here the artifact is pinned and the shipped document is the unpinned one. This is the documentation-provenance gap the repo's own traceability doctrine asks for (row id, spec, test, evidence, verdict), applied to a citation that lives outside the repo.","acceptance_criteria":"0. Reproduce on the current tree: `grep -c 'lane_onboarding\\.go' docs/SCHEDULER-ADMISSION.md` prints `2` and `grep -cE '(bf7427c8|scheduler@[0-9a-f]{7})' docs/SCHEDULER-ADMISSION.md` prints `0`, so the corrected paragraph pins exact line ranges inside another repository while naming no commit that a reader can check them out at. At the scheduler tip this tick measured (`origin/main` = `bf7427c8`) the ranges resolve;