ble`; `grep -A 10 'name = \"code-factory-qa\"' ~/.hermes/fleet.toml | grep -m1 enabled` prints `enabled = true` — the live row and its durable pin disagree, and nothing in the repo records the park.\n1. The repo states which layer owns `enabled` after an auto-disable and where the park is recorded: a documented decision in docs/SCHEDULER-ADMISSION.md naming the auto-disable fields (`disabled_at`, `disabled_by`, `disabled_reason`) and saying whether the durable `fleet.toml` pin is authoritative or is expected to go stale, so a reader can tell without guessing.\n2. After the change the two layers agree, and the row note quotes the re-run of acceptance criterion 0 showing one value for both. If the durable pin is authoritative, the lane is re-enabled and the note names the mechanism and the actor; otherwise the pin is flipped in the same change and the note names where that flip is recorded.\n3. `scripts/load_lane_config.py` refuses to write a lane whose row is parked (`disabled_by` non-empty) unless `--re-arm` is passed, so the only repo-side lane writer cannot silently restore a lane the scheduler parked; the refusal names the lane and writes nothing.\n4. Gate: `bash tests/selftest.sh` prints `SELFTEST: PASS` with rc 0, and the script change carries a selftest arm that goes red without the parked-lane refusal (fixture DB copy, parked row, no `--re-arm`).","probe":"grep -c 'disabled_at' scripts/load_lane_config.py; sqlite3 ~/.hermes/coding-hermes/scheduler.db \"select name, enabled, disabled_by from projects where name='code-factory-qa';\"; grep -A 10 'name = \"code-factory-qa\"' ~/.hermes/fleet.toml | grep -m1 enabled"}
